   "transcript_text": "person_1 (PATIENT) 27.27-30.03s: I'm here because of skipped beats.\nperson_2 (DOCTOR) 30.73-32.68s: when did it begin?\nperson_1 (PATIENT) 34.48-38.15s: It's been like this since last Tuesday.\nperson_2 (DOCTOR) 38.81-41.54s: is it worse with anything in particular?\nperson_1 (PATIENT) 43.13-53.59s: I notice it it more with coffee. I've also had occasional lightheadedness. As for occasional lightheadedness, pretty moderate, if I'm being honest\nperson_2 (DOCTOR) 55.33-57.67s: Let me check your vitals now.\nperson_2 (DOCTOR) 68.33-69.11s: Almost there...\nperson_2 (DOCTOR) 78.12-86.12s: Okay: saturation 99 percent, temp 36.7 degrees, pressure 122 over 73. And heart rate 71.\nperson_2 (DOCTOR) 87.66-90.38s: I'd like to examine you now.\nperson_2 (DOCTOR) 91.37-95.35s: a little pressure here, tell me if it's tender\nperson_2 (DOCTOR) 108.33-114.80s: pulse is regular at the moment, with a couple of early beats while I listened\nperson_2 (DOCTOR) 116.31-119.98s: a little pressure here, tell me if it's tender\nperson_2 (DOCTOR) 128.73-131.91s: Heart sounds are normal with no murmur.\nperson_2 (DOCTOR) 134.73-142.11s: All done, you can get dressed. Let me check a few things. Any fainting or chest pain?\nperson_1 (PATIENT) 147.74-150.10s: Nothing like that, no.\nperson_2 (DOCTOR) 150.89-156.26s: Let me check a few things. Any breathlessness or energy drinks?\nperson_1 (PATIENT) 161.31-165.11s: no breathlessness or energy drinks, none of it\nperson_2 (DOCTOR) 167.10-169.42s: your breathing, any trouble there?\nperson_1 (PATIENT) 170.81-173.65s: No, my breathing's been fine.\nperson_2 (DOCTOR) 175.25-179.38s: What about your tummy and bowels, anything unusual there?\nperson_1 (PATIENT) 180.66-183.35s: No, that side's been fine.\nperson_2 (DOCTOR) 202.28-205.08s: have you had any surgery before?\nperson_1 (PATIENT) 205.93-211.12s: I had a lump removed from my back years ago\nperson_2 (DOCTOR) 226.73-231.02s: Any medication at the moment, including things from the pharmacy?\nperson_3 (FAMILY_MEMBER) 232.14-240.40s: Okay, so she takes Thyronorm, 50 micrograms, every morning. It's escitalopram, 10 milligrams, once a day, doctor.\nperson_2 (DOCTOR) 246.23-251.17s: What about family history, any illnesses that run in the family?\nperson_1 (PATIENT) 252.02-254.36s: My father had a stroke.\nperson_2 (DOCTOR) 255.29-259.71s: what about your general health, any conditions you're being treated for?\nperson_1 (PATIENT) 261.24-271.33s: An underactive thyroid, for nearly five years. It's the not knowing that gets to to me. Yes, anxiety.",
